===Usage notes===
Some usage guides distinguish {{term|farther}} and {{term|further}}, with ''farther'' referring to distance, and ''further'' referring to degree or time.<ref name="grammargirl">[http://grammar.quickanddirtytips.com/further-versus-farther.aspx Grammar Girl:  Further Versus Farther]</ref> Others, such as the OED, recommend ''farther'' as a comparative form of ''far'' and ''further'' for use when it is not comparative.<ref>[http://www.dailywritingtips.com/farther-further-whats-the-difference/ Daily Writing Tips – Farther, Further: What’s the Difference?]</ref>

However, most authorities consider the two interchangeable in most or all circumstances,<ref>[[w:Fowler's Modern English Usage|Fowler’s ''Modern English Usage'']]</ref> and historically they have not been distinguished.<ref name="etyl"/><ref name="grammargirl"/>

